The curve25519 and ed25519 field arithmetic without 64-bit multiplies.
TweetNaCl keeps a field element as sixteen 64-bit limbs and multiplies
them as 64x64 products: GCC makes each a call to libgcc's __muldi3 on
the 68020-68060 (the 68060 has no 64-bit MULU.L, so -m68020-60 never
emits one), 256 calls per field multiply.  The multiply and square now
carry each operand into sixteen signed 16-bit limbs and sum MULS.W
products in 64 bits by add/addx; the reduction by 38 is shifts and adds.
The values are the same mod p, the outputs byte for byte the same, and
it stays constant time.  An x25519 scalar multiply and an ed25519
signature take about a third of the time they did.

--- a/src/curve25519.c
+++ b/src/curve25519.c
